Presence of this file tells GitHub Pages not to run the published output through Jekyll.

It matters for one specific reason. Jekyll excludes files and directories whose names begin with a
dot from its output unless they are named in an `include:` list, and it does so silently, with a
zero exit code and no warning. Verified with Jekyll 4.4.1 on 2026-09-15: a source tree containing
`.well-known/xrp-ledger.toml` built to a `_site` directory with no `.well-known` in it at all.

That path is where the $PND issuer's on-ledger `Domain` field points. Losing it produces a 404 at
the one URL that anchors the token's identity, and nothing in the build output would tell you.

This site is not built by Jekyll, so the exclusion should never apply. This file is here so that it
still cannot apply if someone later switches the repository to a branch-based Pages deployment,
where GitHub runs Jekyll over the branch contents by default.

If you ever do need Jekyll, note that the intuitive fix does not work:

    include: [".well-known/*"]      # verified NOT to work - the file is still dropped
    include: [".well-known"]        # works
    include: [".well-known/xrp-ledger.toml"]   # also works

Either way, verify the deployed result rather than the build log:

    cd site && npm run verify:live -- <domain>
